{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.EquidistantCoordinationCap","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.EquidistantCoordinationCap","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.InnerProductSpace.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Finite","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Lemmas"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.equidistant_card_le_finrank","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.equidistant_card_le_finrank","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquidistantCoordinationCap.lean#L110-L121","doc":"**The cap.** A pairwise-equidistant family of difference vectors in a space\nof finite rank `r` has at most `r` members — so a max-contact cluster of `A`\nnucleons (whose `A − 1` difference vectors are such a family) satisfies\n`A - 1 ≤ r`. In `r = 3` this is `A ≤ 4`: the tetrahedron is the largest\nall-mutually-adjacent point set in ℝ³, and the complete contact graph `K_A` is\n**not 3D-realizable for `A ≥ 5`**. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.linearIndependent_of_equidistant_gram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.linearIndependent_of_equidistant_gram","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquidistantCoordinationCap.lean#L83-L108","doc":"**Equidistant ⇒ linearly independent.**\n\nThe difference vectors of a pairwise-equidistant point family (common distance\n`d ≠ 0`) are linearly independent: `‖Σ cᵢ vᵢ‖²` is `(d²/2)` times a sum of two\nsquares, so it vanishes only when every `cᵢ` does. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.inner_self_sum_of_equidistant_gram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.inner_self_sum_of_equidistant_gram","kind":"theorem","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/EquidistantCoordinationCap.lean#L40-L81","doc":"**The Gram identity for an equidistant family.**\n\nIf `v : Fin n → V` has `⟪v i, v j⟫ = (d²/2)·(1 + δᵢⱼ)` — which is exactly the\nGram matrix of the difference vectors `pᵢ − p₀` of a pairwise-equidistant point\nfamily at common distance `d` — then for any coefficients `c`,\n\n    ‖Σ cᵢ vᵢ‖²  =  (d²/2) · ( (Σ cᵢ)² + Σ cᵢ² ).\n\nBoth bracketed terms are squares, which is what forces independence below. ","signatureChunk":0}]}